/* DATA DESCRIPTION:
Citation:	Abels, Jacqueline; Wedmann, Sonja (2021): List of specimens for geometric morphometric analyses of the wing venation of wasps (Vespinae, Vespidae, Hymenoptera, Insecta) [dataset]. PANGAEA, https://doi.org/10.1594/PANGAEA.937635
Abstract:	A list of Vespinae specimens (Vespidae, Hymenoptera, Insecta) is given in this dataset. The information is related to data of wing landmark coordinates that are archived separately in PANGAEA (see Related to section, Abels & Wedmann 2021). The data were used in the geometric morphometric analyses of Abels & Wedmann (2022). This dataset includes 1025 Vespinae specimens analysed by Perrard et al. (2014, 2015). The following five specimens are added: Vespula rufa (photo "Rødveps Vespula rufa (Linnaeus, 1758)" by Arnstein Staverløkk CC BY-SA 4.0, https://www.artsdatabanken.no/Pages/F21128, accessed 21.02.2021), Vespula nursei (Kumar & Carpenter 2018: Fig. 5), Vespula pensylvanica (Snelling 1981: Fig. 66) and the fossil Palaeovespa socialis (Poinar 2005: Fig. 14) and the species Vespula? hassiaca described in Abels & Wedmann (2022).
Keyword(s):	geometric morphometrics; insects; Vespinae; wing venation landmarks
Supplement to:	Abels, Jacqueline; Wedmann, Sonja (2022): A fossil wasp (Hymenoptera: Vespidae: Vespula? hassiaca) from the Eocene of Messel. Palaeontographica Abteilung A-Paläozoologie-Stratigraphie, 323(1-3), 105-117, https://doi.org/10.1127/pala/2021/0122
Related to:	Abels, Jacqueline; Wedmann, Sonja (2021): Data for geometric morphometric analyses of the wing venation of wasps (Vespinae, Vespidae, Hymenoptera, Insecta) [dataset]. PANGAEA, https://doi.org/10.1594/PANGAEA.937651
	Kumar, P Girish; Carpenter, James M (2018): A Taxonomic Review Of The Genus Vespula Thomson (Hymenoptera: Vespidae: Vespinae) From The Indian Subcontinent. Zenodo, Halteres, 9, 86-110, https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.1258043
	Perrard, Adrien; Baylac, Michel; Carpenter, James M; Villemant, Claire (2014): Evolution of wing shape in hornets: why is the wing venation efficient for species identification? Journal of Evolutionary Biology, 27(12), 2665-2675, https://doi.org/10.1111/jeb.12523
	Perrard, Adrien; Baylac, Michel; Carpenter, James M; Villemant, Claire (2015): Data from: Evolution of wing shape in hornets: why is the wing venation efficient for species identification [dataset]? Dryad, https://doi.org/10.5061/dryad.2cv1h
	Poinar, George (2005): Fossil Trigonalidae and Vespidae (Hymenoptera) in Baltic amber. Proceedings of the Entomological Society of Washington, 107(1), 55-63
	Snelling, Roy R (1981): Systematics of social Hymenoptera. In: Hermann, HR (editor), Social insects. New York: Academic Press, 2, 369-453
Parameter(s):	Specimen number (Spec no) * PI: Abels, Jacqueline (jacqueline.abels@t-online.de)
